From 675514884a91e88df0040a9be050392953705407 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 21:01:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] chore(state): log turn-lease custody events so a lost lease
 names its producer [daveey]

Investigation tooling for t_eeeeee000000002b, not a fix. A running Desktop turn was refused with
'missing lease row' 3.8 s after admission (session 20000101_000000_00000b) and no log said which
statement removed the row, or whether it was ever readable after the acquire returned.

Every statement that inserts, deletes or refuses on session_turn_leases now logs one bounded line
on hermes_state.lease_custody: claim (real INSERT rowcount and whether the holder owns the row),
release / worker release / worker cleanup (only when a row was removed), stale reclaim on acquire
and by a lease-less writer, the long-expired sweep (tags of the rows removed), and missing /
mismatch refusals (WARNING, with lease-table row count and whether the session row and a parent
exist). _execute_write settles each attempt as committed, rolled_back or commit_failed under the
same txn tag, so an in-transaction event is never read as a commit. Admission logs a WARNING when
the reader connection cannot see the row the acquire just reported.

Keys, holders, session ids and the store path appear only as 12-hex SHA-256 tags. Nothing here
inserts, renews or restores a lease row; a logging failure changes no admission and no refusal.

Proof: tests/hermes_state/test_turn_lease_custody_events.py 9/9 (lifecycle with settlement and
redaction, never-admitted / superseded / released holders still refused, rollback, sweep, both
reclaims, worker release, distinct stores, admission readback, failing logger). 313 lease-related
test files: 5108 passed, 0 failed. scripts/check: 21 checks ok.
